QSM Direct: optional TCP 8006 gateway (experimental, single-node PVE 9)
===================================================================

This explicit profile shares TCP 8006 between normal PVE HTTPS and authenticated
TURN over TCP. Signalling goes through /qsm-direct/ on the same HTTPS origin;
video, audio and input use WebRTC DTLS/SRTP/SCTP inside TURN/TCP. The browser must
be able to open arbitrary TCP to 8006, not just HTTP requests through a web proxy.
TURN itself is plain TCP; media remains encrypted by WebRTC. For networks that
require TLS for every connection, use a separately configured TURN/TLS service.
TCP retransmission can add latency under packet loss; direct UDP remains the
normal profile for networks that allow it.

Requirements and activation
---------------------------
Install matching core, standalone and gateway debs from the release. LXC setup
still needs the separate LXC helper and container guest package.

HAProxy, coturn and nftables binaries are needed. They are Suggested rather than
hard dependencies: installing their generic services blindly can create extra
listeners or disturb an existing operator-managed service. If NONE of these
services is already installed/configured, install without starting their generic
units:

  systemctl mask --runtime haproxy.service coturn.service
  apt-get install haproxy coturn nftables
  systemctl disable haproxy.service coturn.service
  systemctl unmask --runtime haproxy.service coturn.service

For existing installations keep their services/configuration intact and install
only missing binaries using your normal service-management procedure. QSM uses
its own units, private configuration and dedicated qsm-turn user.

During a maintenance window (pveproxy and signalling restart):

  qsm-pve-direct-gateway enable --relay-ip <this-node-non-loopback-IPv4>

Reload the PVE page. The endpoint uses the browser's actual HTTPS origin/port,
including a forwarded/NAT port. On a directly exposed node it is TCP 8006.
HTTPS, normal PVE API/WebSocket traffic and TURN/TCP share that listener on
the specified node IPv4 address and on IPv6. Other node IPv4 addresses are not
automatically exposed by this profile.
Backend listeners 18006, 18007, 13479 and 13480 bind ONLY to loopback. No firewall
INPUT/FORWARD rule is added and no global firewall table is flushed.

Scope and security
------------------
Automatic setup accepts only single-node stock PVE and an absent/comment-only
/etc/default/pveproxy. Custom pveproxy settings require manual review, including
LISTEN_IP, ALLOW_FROM, DENY_FROM, POLICY and real-IP proxy settings. It does not
silently bypass an existing source-address ACL. Cluster forwarding, source-IP
access policies and use with an existing reverse proxy require manual review;
they are not covered by automatic setup. The native patched viewer is not covered by this gateway
profile; the bitrate fix is shared by both installation profiles. It changes /etc/default/pveproxy so the
original HTTPS daemon binds to loopback, and sets its trusted real-IP header.
PVE also uses LISTEN_IP for spiceproxy: external SPICE is affected in this mode.
Stock Proxmox binaries/packages are not replaced.

The TCP multiplexer passes client addresses to both HTTPS and coturn using
PROXY protocol v2. This also avoids reporting a loopback mapped address to
Firefox. Coturn's PROXY listener is private, never directly exposed.

The gateway issues ten-minute TURN REST credentials only after PVE verifies
VM.Console on the requested guest. Credentials contain no PVE ticket or username.
Coturn permits only this node's configured IPv4 address. A separate nftables
OUTPUT table further permits its dedicated UID to send UDP only to the exact
currently bound QSM ICE socket tuples. Leases refresh every five seconds and
expire after fifteen seconds if a terminal crashes; a failed refresh closes the
peer. Arbitrary node/network UDP services cannot be used as relay destinations.
Normal sessions remove their leases before closing sockets. The table is
recreated empty at boot; generic coturn users are unaffected.

The gateway keeps the node's PVE/custom certificate, verifies both local HTTPS
backends, and reloads the frontend within a minute after a certificate update.
No PVE passwords, tickets, SDP or TURN secrets are logged by these tools.

Disable and rollback
--------------------
  qsm-pve-direct-gateway disable

This stops/disables QSM gateway units, deletes only its OUTPUT table, restores
prior pveproxy and signal configuration byte-for-byte and restarts pveproxy.
It restores the prior signalling active/enabled state. Removing an active gateway
package performs the same rollback. Keep /var/lib/qsm-pve-direct-gateway/before.json
until disable succeeds; it is the rollback record. Enabling is explicit after
installation; upgrading the gateway tools does not enable a disabled profile.
